{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791565667,"nonce":"JChXJ2jHTl3CdXGtAp1T3XTzxZU=","title":"Overloads across a class hierarchy and interfaces are not selected (TestOverloads)","message":"`overload` functions are renamed apart per owner by declaration order (`name__ovl\u003ci\u003e`, tast/ast_lowering/overloads.rs) and a call picks the member from its argument types (call_overload.rs). That covers static, member, module-level and `using` overloads (TestOverloadsForEveryone, Issue9737, Issue10205, Issue10955).\n\nIt cannot cover a hierarchy: an `override overload` in a subclass, or an interface declaring overloads, would get a different index than the member it overrides or implements, so dispatch pairs the wrong bodies. TestOverloads (BaseJava/ChildJava/OverloadedInterface `someField`, overloaded constructors) is that case; files mixing `overload` with `override` or with an overloading interface are left unrenamed.\n\nNeeds: names derived from the parameter signature so an override and an interface member land on the same name; a call resolving against the owner's whole ancestry; overloaded constructors (`new` is excluded today).","files":null}]}